t(63, 820, """Middle Eastern culture .. the Kurds of Syria .. the changes

The religious education of the Kurds in Syria has witnessed radical transformations over the past fifty years, moving from strict government dominance and traditional Sufi education (during the rule of the Ba'ath Party) to a multi-religious secular educational system taught by the Autonomous Administration of North and East Syria after the events of 2011.

With the retreat of the Syrian government's control over the regions of north and east Syria (the Kurdish-majority region) after 2011 and the founding of the "Autonomous Administration", the concept of religious education changed completely:
Changing the curricula: The Autonomous Administration adopted its own educational curricula, radically different from the Damascus curriculum.
Shift to a neutral (secular) system: The Islamic character guiding public schools was removed, and local government schools came to adopt religious neutrality.
Introducing a "Beliefs of Peoples" subject: Instead of the traditional Islamic education subject, a subject was introduced concerned with teaching the different religions and sects prevalent in the region fairly and tolerantly. It covers the principles of Islam, Yazidism and Christianity.
Education in the mother tongue: Religious and cultural education came to be taught in Kurdish for the first time in the official schools under the Autonomous Administration.
Religious freedom for Kurdish minorities: Yazidi Kurds (an important Kurdish religious minority in al-Jazira and Afrin) were allowed to learn and spread their religion freely, after decades of marginalisation or being forced into the Islamic curriculum in government schools.""")

h(63, 2200, """The Middle East. A look at the culture of the Kurds of Syria: the religious influence""")

